% ============================================================================
% meniscus-reading-point — for each of the two basic shapes a liquid's
% meniscus can take inside a laboratory measuring vessel (concave or convex),
% WHICH point of its curve a scientist actually reads to take the
% measurement, a fresh WebFetch-sourced NIST table (adj-facts-stdlib,
% CHEMISTRY). Grounds the "observation and measurement" Major Gap
% ADJ-STDLIB-COVERAGE.md §5.1/§5.2 names for K-8 science — the SECOND
% instance of that gap (after `chemistry/measuring-tool-si-unit.adj`), and
% the first to ground it with a fresh source rather than composing two
% already-shipped tables (composition was confirmed exhaustively dry for
% this gap two sessions prior).
% ============================================================================
% `chemistry/measuring-tool-si-unit.adj` already tables WHICH internationally
% standardized SI unit a lab tool's own reading is expressed in (a
% tool-to-unit mapping); `chemistry/measuring-tools.adj` already tables WHICH
% quantity a tool measures. Neither touches a genuinely different axis of
% "observation and measurement": once you have the right tool pointed at the
% right quantity, HOW do you actually read its scale correctly? For any
% liquid held in a narrow tube (a graduated cylinder, a burette, a
% volumetric flask), surface tension pulls the liquid's surface into a
% curve — a meniscus — and that curve is not flat, so "read the number where
% the liquid's surface is" is ambiguous until you know WHICH point of the
% curve counts. NIST's own "Good Measurement Practice for Method of Reading
% a Meniscus" (GMP 3, a National Institute of Standards and Technology
% Interagency Report, a source never before cited in this stdlib) states the
% rule for each of the two shapes directly, in two back-to-back paragraphs:
%
%   "The meniscus of most liquids used in volumetric calibrations is
%    concave-up, with the lowest point in the center that is used to
%    determine the meniscus reading."
%
%   "The meniscus formed by a non-wetting liquid, such as mercury (Hg), is
%    convex with the highest point in the center and is no longer regularly
%    used for volumetric calibrations. In the case of a convex meniscus, the
%    highest point is used to make the reading."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? meniscus_reading_point(concave, $Point)   % lowest_point
%     ? meniscus_reading_point(convex, $Point)    % highest_point
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `meniscus_reading_point(meniscus_shape, reading_point)` carrying
% the citation, so the lookups above are ordinary SLD binding queries — the
% engine returns the reading point WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on a meniscus shape the source does not name.
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a reading point and recall which shape
% it belongs to:
%
%     ? meniscus_reading_point($Shape, lowest_point)    % concave
%     ? meniscus_reading_point($Shape, highest_point)   % convex
%
% The two meniscus shapes and their reading point, as a little truth table:
%
%     meniscus_shape   reading_point    the source's own words
%     ---------------  ---------------  --------------------------------------
%     concave          lowest_point     "concave-up, with the lowest point
%                                        ... used to determine the reading"
%     convex           highest_point    "convex with the highest point in
%                                        the center ... the highest point is
%                                        used to make the reading"
%
% WHY this is a genuine "observation and measurement" fact, not a
% coincidental relabeling of anything already shipped: reading a meniscus
% wrong (top instead of bottom, or vice versa) is a real, named source of
% systematic measurement error — the SAME NIST document devotes its later
% sections to quantifying "Uncertainty and Error Analysis in Meniscus
% Readings" precisely because this convention is easy to get backwards. That
% is a different competency than knowing WHICH tool measures WHICH quantity
% (`measuring-tools.adj`) or WHICH SI unit a tool's reading reduces to
% (`measuring-tool-si-unit.adj`) — it is the actual reading TECHNIQUE, the
% step between "the liquid is somewhere near the 50 mL line" and "the volume
% is 50.0 mL."
%
% HONEST ABSTENTION — the crux of why this table is narrow rather than
% overreaching: the cited document discusses exactly two meniscus shapes,
% concave and convex, and states no third shape (a "flat" or perfectly level
% liquid surface is never named as a real case this document covers). A
% query for a meniscus shape the source does not name — e.g. `flat` —
% honestly abstains rather than inventing a reading point. The document's
% own named EXAMPLE of a convex-meniscus liquid, mercury, is deliberately
% NOT promoted to its own row or column here: the two rows above are keyed
% on the SHAPE (concave/convex), the axis the source's own two topic
% sentences state the reading rule against, not on a specific liquid — the
% document itself notes the convex/mercury case "is no longer regularly
% used for volumetric calibrations," so a scientist reading a modern
% graduated cylinder or burette almost always faces the concave case, and
% the mercury example is kept in this header's prose (and in the `cites`
% span below, verbatim) rather than turned into unstated new content.
%
% Provenance: WebFetch-verified directly against the raw document text
% (a NIST Interagency Report PDF, not an HTML page) before writing this
% file, confirming both quoted spans byte-for-byte, including the absence
% of any third meniscus shape anywhere in the document's seven pages.
% `https://www.nist.gov/system/files/documents/2019/05/14/gmp-3-reading-meniscus-20190507.pdf`
% ("GMP 3 – Good Measurement Practice for Method of Reading a Meniscus
% Using Water or Other Wetting Liquid", NIST Interagency Report
% NIST.IR.7383-2019, DOI https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.IR.7383-2019) — a
% nist.gov (.gov) primary government reference, the same standing this
% stdlib already gives `metrology/si-base-units.adj`'s NIST citation.
% `trust authoritative`. Nothing here is asserted from memory. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table meniscus_reading_point {
    columns meniscus_shape, reading_point

    row (concave, lowest_point)
    row (convex, highest_point)

    source "The meniscus of most liquids used in volumetric calibrations is concave-up, with the lowest point in the center that is used to determine the meniscus reading."
    locator "https://www.nist.gov/system/files/documents/2019/05/14/gmp-3-reading-meniscus-20190507.pdf"
    trust authoritative

    cites "The meniscus formed by a non-wetting liquid, such as mercury (Hg), is convex with the highest point in the center and is no longer regularly used for volumetric calibrations. In the case of a convex meniscus, the highest point is used to make the reading." locator "https://www.nist.gov/system/files/documents/2019/05/14/gmp-3-reading-meniscus-20190507.pdf"
}
